 Youth Programs Inclusion Inclusion Everyone Belongs at the University of New Haven The Myatt Center Accessibility Resources Center Graduate and International Student Life IDEA Council Military & Veteran Affairs Spiritual Life and Campus Ministry Title IX Alumni & Friends Alumni & Friends Our Alumni Alumni Events Alumni Involvement Alumni Benefits Magazine Alumni Association Contact Alumni Relations Support the University Research Research Research Overview Labs & Groups Faculty Research Student Fellowships & Research Summer Undergraduate Research Fellowships (SURF) Grants & Sponsored Programs Explore Explore Explore the University West Haven & Beyond News and Media Events Camps and Conferences Community Spotlights The Arts on Campus Digital Downloads Athletics Athletics Social Media Instagram Facebook Tik Tok LinkedIn X YouTube Close Menu Section Menu Research / Faculty Research Research in Engineering, Computer Science, and Chemistry Main Page Chemistry & Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Research Main Page Synthesis of 1-AZA-Bicyclic Compounds Synthesis of Cadiolides and Cadiolide Analogs Synthesis of Dyes for Dye-Sensitized Solar Cells Electrical & Computer Engineering Research Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Research Main Page Use of Computational Fluid Dynamics for the Design and Optimization of Deceleration Devices Phase Change Materials (PCMs) for Thermal Energy Storage Tri-Sol Solar Energy Cellular and Molecular Biology Research Technology Transfer and Intellectual Property Main Page Information for Faculty and Inventors Information for Industry Documents and Forms Frequently Asked Questions Close Faculty Research Scholars & Experts Beyond being gifted educators, University of New Haven faculty are world-class researchers. This provides limitless opportunities for them to bring their research into the classroom and to collaborate with you on their next big breakthrough. Apply Request Info University of New Haven faculty members are widely recognized as experts in their fields. The depth and breadth of their work is second to none, creating transformational learning opportunities for all students. Research in Engineering, Computer Science, and Chemistry Whether your plans for the future involve taking your research expertise into industry or continuing your research activities in the academic sphere, the Tagliatela College of Engineering will prepare you to meet tomorrow’s challenges through participation in some of the most exciting, productive — and relevant — projects in the country. Learn More Cellular and Molecular Biology Faculty Research Interests The University of New Haven established the Lyme disease research group in 2005. To date, more than 70 graduate students have received training in Lyme disease-related research. Learn More Forensic Science Faculty Research Forensic Science Research at the University of New Haven reflects the breadth of its faculty, who are also renowned subject matter experts. Starting with technologies used at the incident scene and reconstructive applications, through the laboratory-based disciplines, and incorporating novel technologies such as nucleic acid imaging and forensic investigative genetic genealogy, faculty research impacts foundational understanding, operational practices, investigative clearance rates, and informs policy. Learn More Technology Transfer and Intellectual Property The University of New Haven technology transfer team is responsible for the identification, protection, marketing, and licensing of intellectual assets developed by University of New Haven faculty, staff and, in some cases, students. Learn More Spotlights MELISSA WHITSON, PH.D. Interdisciplinary projects create opportunities for groundbreaking research CHARLES \"ANDY\" MORGAN III, M.D.,M.A. Authority on PTSD questions validity of eyewitness testimony DEQUAN XIAO, PH.D. Chemists contribute to leukemia drug discovery All Spotlights University of New Haven 300 Boston Post Road West Haven, 